  #text(weight: "bold", size: 14pt)[
    Substitution, Slack, and Scarcity: Navigating Paradigmatic Divides in Input-Output Analysis for Socio-ecological Transformation
  ]
  #v(8pt)
  Franz Scharnreitner#h(1em)Jakob Kapeller#h(1em)Johannes Kepler Universität Linz
]

// Abstract block
#v(12pt)
#block(
  fill: luma(240),
  inset: 8pt,
  radius: 4pt,
)[
A famous assertion in Thomas Kuhn’s account of the structure of scientific revolutions posits that researchers devoted to different theoretical frameworks—or, in Kuhn’s terms, associated with different paradigms—will tend to have conflicting interpretations of the available empirical data.
The key reason for this lies in what is sometimes called the ‘theory-ladenness’ of observation, i.e., the fact that our theoretical presuppositions are implicitly or explicitly employed when making sense of observed data, relationships, or patterns.

Economics abounds with examples of such Kuhnian cleavages regarding the correct, or at least unbiased, interpretation of empirical data:
The origins of unemployment (are wages too high or too low?), behavioral patterns in economic decision-making (do these represent rational heuristics or biases that document a deviation from the generally valid standard model of rational action?), or the interpretation of the climate crisis (as either a maximization problem or a maximin problem) provide relevant cases of such Kuhnian cleavages in economics.

Against this backdrop, we contrast the CES general equilibrium (CES-GE) approach with the classical Leontief input-output (IO) model, highlighting key differences in substitution flexibility, price formation, and feedback mechanisms.
Furthermore, we explore the differences in paradigms that underlie both approaches and develop ways to bridge them, extending the models presented in Baqaee and Farhi (2019 and 2022).
We introduce methods to incorporate labor slack into the CES-GE model and analyze the implications of different choices of substitution elasticities.
Using the example of required large-scale investments in housing and railroads in Germany, this paper compares the more traditional Leontief-based approaches to input-output tables—where additional investment leads to expansion—with mainstream CGE models, where such additional spending typically causes contraction due to scarce primary factors.
We take this case as an opportunity to explore possibilities for effectively bridging such a Kuhnian cleavage in an applied context.
]
